# pico-cam-a-platform

Waveshare PICO-Cam-A board, LCD and HM01B0 camera providers.

The original HM01B0 camera support code is ArduCAM's RPI-Pico-Cam:
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam

The upstream camera implementation and sensor initialization header are in
rp2040_hm01b0/arducam:
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/tree/master/rp2040_hm01b0/arducam
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/blob/master/rp2040_hm01b0/arducam/hm01b0_init.h

The upstream PIO program is rp2040_hm01b0/image.pio:
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/blob/master/rp2040_hm01b0/image.pio

This integration used the HM01B0 table and camera example supplied in
Waveshare's PICO-Cam-A support archive, which adapts that ArduCAM code:
https://files.waveshare.com/wiki/PICO-Cam-A/PICO-Cam-A.zip

camera/hm01b0_init.h retains the original filename and the notice from the
Waveshare distribution. Its table is adapted for this integration: the adapter
performs software reset separately, the sentinel is omitted in favor of the
array length. All 77 non-reset register writes now match the working Waveshare
demo, including square output (0x3010=0). The adapter restores the demo's 10
ms delay after every initialization write, including reset and stream-on. The
original gated, MSB-first serial setting 0x3060=0x30 is retained. The
Waveshare version's other register settings are retained.

camera/capture.inc.c adapts the upstream PIO/DMA capture approach for this
board and the modules.cpp interface, adding timeouts, frame synchronization in
PIO, sensor identity checks, resource allocation, and cleanup. It is an
adaptation of the original support approach, not a claim of original camera
support authorship. The C++ provider and portable mm.camera interface are
integration code; the LCD panel settings come from Waveshare's LCD example.

## Live preview controls

apps/camera-demo starts capture automatically in CROP after reset, showing
WAIT during exposure settling and then LIVE. K3 (GP18) stops/restarts capture; K4 (GP19)
cycles CROP (default), NEAREST, AREA, and AREA+TIME, including while paused.
Both buttons are active-low, watched with pull-ups and release debouncing. The
LCD shows state and mode; stopping retains the last image and releases camera
resources. Eight startup frames are discarded before showing video. CROP uses
the reference's x=0..239 and reversed y=134..0 window; unlike its loop, row
zero is also written. Resizing modes reverse their output rows consistently.
Image and status are composed into one 240x135 RGB565 transfer, rather than
separate row writes. Temporal averaging resets on restart or mode change.

The mm.camera.preview processing and controls, and its tests under
tests/mm/camera/preview/, are new modules.cpp module/test code, not upstream
camera support code. Run the C++20 host test with sh test.sh --host
tests/mm/camera/preview/. It checks weighted area sampling against a
reference, temporal history, button bounce, held buttons, taps latched during
capture, and timer wraparound. It does not measure image quality or verify
physical button behavior.

## Capture timing audit

ArduCAM's image.pio waits for LVLD, waits for PCLK high, samples one bit, and
waits for PCLK low. Its ST7735 demo sets clk_sys to 250 MHz and uses PWM wrap
9 for a 25 MHz MCLK, giving 10 PIO cycles per serial bit. Its base example at
125 MHz uses a 12.5 MHz PWM MCLK, again 10 cycles per bit. The PWM comment
mentioning wrap 5 is stale; the executable code uses wrap 9.

PICO-Cam-A has a fixed 36 MHz oscillator, so GPIO PWM cannot slow its MCLK.
The previous three-instruction loop at 125 MHz had only 3.47 PIO cycles per
bit: WAIT-high recognition followed by IN could sample after the bit's valid
window (HM01B0 Figure 6.5). Instruction throughput alone was not a valid
timing guarantee. The previous 0x3060=0x20 setting also incorrectly cleared
msb_en; it is restored to ArduCAM/Waveshare's 0x30, as specified in HM01B0
Table 6.6 and register 0x3060.

The capture loop now matches ArduCAM's LVLD-high, PCLK-high, IN, PCLK-low
sequence, with the existing PIO FVLD synchronization prefix. Camera-linked
applications set the RP2040 core regulator to 1.15 V, wait 10 ms, then set
clk_sys to 250 MHz before C++ initialization or application peripheral
configuration, matching the working PICO-Cam-A/C/main.c capture clock.
Waveshare uses 1.10 V; this integration retains 1.15 V. Raspberry Pi's RP2040
datasheet section 2.15.3 rates 200 MHz at 1.15 V; 250 MHz is an overclock and
is not a datasheet-qualified operating point. It provides 6.94 PIO cycles per
fixed 36 MHz bit, versus the previous 5.56 at 200 MHz. The adapter refuses
initialization or capture below 250 MHz. The clock setup is linked only when
the camera provider is reached, and remains in effect when the camera sleeps.
No runtime clock change occurs on K3 presses. PIO allocation now requires six
instructions.

Physical timing, signal integrity and image quality require board testing.
AREA+TIME can produce a shifted ghost of moving objects because it blends
current output with previous frames; CROP, NEAREST and AREA do not blend frames.

Timing sources:
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/blob/master/rp2040_hm01b0/image.pio
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/blob/master/rp2040_hm01b0/arducam/arducam.c
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/blob/master/rp2040_hm01b0_st7735/main.c
https://github.com/ArduCAM/RPI-Pico-Cam/blob/master/rp2040_hm01b0_st7735/arducam/arducam.c
https://files.waveshare.com/wiki/common/HM01B0-datasheet.pdf
https://pip-assets.raspberrypi.com/categories/814-rp2040/documents/RP-008371-DS-1-rp2040-datasheet.pdf

## Comparison with the working Waveshare C demo

Compared directly with ../Waveshare/PICO-Cam-A/C/lib/CAM/cam.c,
lib/CAM/hm01b0_init.h, lib/CAM/image.pio and main.c. The 77 non-reset
sensor register writes match in order and value: automatic exposure,
gain limits, black-level correction, defect correction, line/frame lengths,
serial mode and orientation. Reset is still performed separately, with its
10 ms delay. Register transfers remain bounded hardware I2C at 100 kHz
rather than the demo's bit-banged SCCB. Frame synchronization remains in
PIO, with FIFO clearing and timeout recovery on each capture.

The default crop follows main.c's display window and vertical order, but fills
all 135 rows instead of leaving the last row unwritten. LCD porch, power,
gamma, inversion, MADCTL and offsets already match the reference. The SPI
request now also matches its 100 MHz request; the Pico divider selects 62.5
MHz at a 250 MHz system clock. Each display update transfers one full RGB565
frame. The reference captures on core 1 while core 0 sends LCD data, sharing a
buffer without synchronization. This integration keeps the single-core
synchronous camera API and a stable complete frame during LCD transfer; equal
frame rate to the two-core reference is not claimed.
